window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.3","title":"Errors and Power","goal":"Simulate thousands of experiments to see the Type I error rate when nothing is going on and the power when a real 6-point effect exists, then check with a power calculator.","question":"A school wonders whether a new teaching method raises exam scores. Past exams have a standard deviation of 15 points, and the method might add about 6 points. The plan is to compare two groups of 36 students with a t-test. If the method really works, how likely is this study to notice it, and how many students per group would we need to be fairly sure?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Plan the experiment","text":"H0: the two groups have the same average score. H1: they differ. The method may add 6 points (SD 15), and we plan 36 students per group with a t-test at alpha = 0.05. The effect size d is the gap in SDs: 6 / 15 = 0.4.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nn, sd, diff, alpha, reps = 36, 15, 6, 0.05, 10_000\n\nd = diff / sd\nprint(f'effect size d = {d:.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy for random numbers and arrays, under the short name np.","Load SciPy's statistics tools; we need its t-test.","Create a seeded random number generator, so every run gives the same simulated experiments.","Store the plan in one line: 36 per group, SD 15, a real gap of 6 points, alpha 0.05, and 10,000 pretend experiments.","Effect size d: the gap measured in standard deviations. It lets us compare effects on any scale.","Print d, to rounded 2 decimals, as a check of the plan."],"meaning":"d = 0.40 means the method moves scores by 0.4 of a standard deviation, a small-to-medium effect. A study plan always starts from a guess like this: you cannot talk about power without saying how big an effect you hope to find.","out":"effect size d = 0.40","figs":[]},{"title":"Simulate when the null is true","text":"We run 10,000 pretend experiments where both groups come from the same population. The share of experiments that still reject H0 is the Type I error rate, and it should be close to alpha.","code":"def reject_rate(true_diff):\n    a = rng.normal(0, sd, size=(reps, n))\n    b = rng.normal(true_diff, sd, size=(reps, n))\n    p = stats.ttest_ind(a, b, axis=1).pvalue   # one t-test per row\n    return (p <= alpha).mean()\n\ntype1 = reject_rate(0)\nprint(f'Type I error rate (no real effect) = {type1:.3f}')","explain":["Define a reusable function that simulates 10,000 experiments for a given true gap and returns how often the t-test rejects.","Make 10,000 rows of 36 scores for group A (mean 0). Each row is one pretend experiment.","Make 10,000 rows of 36 scores for group B, shifted up by the true gap.","Run one t-test per row (axis=1) and keep all the p-values.","The share of p-values at or below alpha is the share of experiments that reject H0.","Run it with a true gap of 0, so H0 is really true.","Print the rejection rate, which is the Type I error rate (false alarms)."],"meaning":"With no real effect, 4.9% of the 10,000 experiments still gave p <= 0.05. That is the Type I error rate, and it is close to alpha = 0.05, as theory says. About 1 in 20 studies of a useless method will look significant by pure luck.","out":"Type I error rate (no real effect) = 0.049","figs":[]},{"title":"Simulate when a real effect exists","text":"Now the second group truly scores 6 points higher. The share of experiments that reject H0 is the power. With only 36 per group the test misses the effect more often than it finds it.","code":"power_sim = reject_rate(diff)\n\nprint(f'simulated power = {power_sim:.3f}')\nprint(f'simulated Type II error (beta) = {1 - power_sim:.3f}')","explain":["Run the same function with a true 6-point gap: now H0 is false, so the rejection rate is the power.","Print the power: the share of experiments that correctly found the real effect.","Print beta, the Type II error rate: the share that missed it. It is 1 minus power."],"meaning":"When the method truly adds 6 points, only 38.7% of the experiments detect it (power = 0.387). The other 61.3% miss it (Type II error). So with 36 students per group, a real 6-point gain is missed more often than it is found.","out":"simulated power = 0.387\nsimulated Type II error (beta) = 0.613","figs":[]},{"title":"Check with statsmodels and plan the sample size","text":"TTestIndPower does the calculation exactly, without simulation. power() gives the power for n = 36 per group, and solve_power() finds the group size needed for 80% power.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.power import TTestIndPower\n\nanalysis = TTestIndPower()\npower_exact = analysis.power(effect_size=d, nobs1=n, alpha=alpha)\nn_needed = analysis.solve_power(effect_size=d, power=0.80, alpha=alpha)\n\nprint(f'exact power with {n} per group = {power_exact:.3f}')\nprint(f'n per group for 80% power = {n_needed:.2f}, so use {int(np.ceil(n_needed))}')","explain":["Load statsmodels' power calculator for two-sample t-tests.","Create the calculator object. It can answer power or sample-size questions without simulation.","power() gives the exact chance of rejecting H0 for our effect size, 36 per group and alpha.","solve_power() runs the calculation backwards: how many per group do we need for 80% power?","Print the exact power for the planned study.","Print the needed group size; sample sizes must be whole people, so we round up."],"meaning":"The exact power is 0.388, matching the simulated 0.387 (the small difference is random noise). To reach the usual target of 80% power we need 99.08 students per group, so we recruit 100 per group. Power depends on effect size, spread, alpha and sample size, and the sample size is the lever we control.","out":"exact power with 36 per group = 0.388\nn per group for 80% power = 99.08, so use 100","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"power.t.test(n = 36, delta = 6, sd = 15)                # power for 36 per group\npower.t.test(power = 0.80, delta = 6, sd = 15)           # n per group for 80% power\n\n# simulation (random, so close to but not exactly the Python value)\nset.seed(42)\nmean(replicate(10000, t.test(rnorm(36, 0, 15), rnorm(36, 0, 15))$p.value <= 0.05))\nmean(replicate(10000, t.test(rnorm(36, 0, 15), rnorm(36, 6, 15))$p.value <= 0.05))","explain":["power.t.test computes power for a two-sample t-test: 36 per group, a real gap (delta) of 6 and an SD of 15. Leaving out power makes R solve for it.","Leave out n and give power = 0.80: R solves for the group size needed.","A comment (R ignores it). The next lines are a simulation, so numbers will be close to Python's, not identical.","Fix R's random numbers so the simulation repeats.","Simulate 10,000 experiments with no real gap; t.test()$p.value <= 0.05 marks rejections and mean() gives the Type I error rate.","Same simulation with a true gap of 6; the rejection rate is the power."],"expect":"power = 0.3875 for n = 36 per group; n = 99.08 per group for 80% power (use 100); simulated rates about 0.05 and about 0.39","read":"power.t.test prints power = 0.3875 for 36 per group, and n = 99.08 per group for 80% power (use 100). The two simulated rates should be near 0.05 (false alarms) and near 0.39 (power), but not exactly the same as Python's.","note":"power.t.test is exact for a two-sample, two-sided t-test, and n is per group. The two simulated rates change a little with every seed and will not match Python's digit for digit."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Power Analysis > Means > Independent-Samples T Test (enter power 0.80 to solve for the sample size)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / n delta sd.\nBEGIN DATA\n36 6 15\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE df = 2 * n - 2.\nCOMPUTE nc = (delta / sd) * SQRT(n / 2).\nCOMPUTE tcrit = IDF.T(0.975, df).\nCOMPUTE power = 1 - NCDF.T(tcrit, df, nc) + NCDF.T(-tcrit, df, nc).\nLIST power.","explain":["Define three variables: the group size n, the true gap delta and the SD.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","One data row: 36 per group, a gap of 6 and an SD of 15.","Marks the end of the data.","Degrees of freedom for two groups of n: 2n - 2.","The noncentral parameter: the gap in SDs times sqrt(n/2). It says how far the real t distribution sits from the null one.","The two-sided critical t at alpha = 0.05, from the usual t distribution.","Power is the chance the noncentral t falls beyond either critical value, computed with NCDF.T.","LIST prints the power."],"read":"LIST shows power as about 0.39 (SPSS rounds the display; the exact value is 0.3875). That means a real 6-point gain would be detected only about 39% of the time with 36 per group. Use the Power Analysis menu to solve for the 100 per group needed.","note":"The Power Analysis menu is the easy way and can also solve for n. The syntax shows the same exact power by hand, using the noncentral t distribution. SPSS has no built-in simulation here."},"stata":{"code":"power twomeans 0 6, sd(15) n1(36) n2(36)      // power for 36 per group\npower twomeans 0 6, sd(15) power(0.80)         // sample size for 80% power","explain":["power twomeans takes the two group means (0 and 6), the common SD and the group sizes, and reports the power.","Give a power of 0.80 instead of the sample sizes, and Stata solves for the sample size."],"read":"The first table shows Estimated power = 0.3875 for N1 = N2 = 36. The second shows the sample size for 80% power, N1 = N2 = 100 per group (Stata rounds up from 99.08), matching the Python and R results.","note":"power twomeans takes the two group means (0 and 6) and the common SD. Stata reports whole group sizes, so it rounds the 99.08 up to 100 per group."},"conclusion":"With 36 students per group the study would detect a real 6-point gain only about 39% of the time (exact power 0.388), so a non-significant result would prove little. To have an 80% chance of detecting it we need about 100 per group. Caution: these numbers assume the effect really is 6 points with SD 15, and real effects are often smaller than hoped.","practice":{"scenario":"A website team wants to test whether a new layout lengthens the average visit by 3 minutes. Past visits have a standard deviation of about 10 minutes. They plan 40 visitors per group and a t-test at alpha = 0.05. Is the plan strong enough? Find the effect size, the power, the group size needed for 80% power and a quick simulation check.","data":"diff, sd, n, alpha = 3, 10, 40, 0.05   # minutes gained, spread, group size, significance level","tasks":["Compute the effect size d.","Find the exact power for 40 per group.","Find the group size needed for 80% power (round up).","Simulate 5,000 experiments with a real 3-minute gain and compare the rejection rate with the exact power."],"hint":"Use TTestIndPower from statsmodels: .power(...) for the first question and .solve_power(...) for the second. The simulation looks like the lab's reject_rate function.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom statsmodels.stats.power import TTestIndPower\n\ndiff, sd, n, alpha = 3, 10, 40, 0.05\nd = diff / sd\ntool = TTestIndPower()\nprint(f'effect size d = {d:.2f}')\nprint(f'power with {n} per group = {tool.power(d, nobs1=n, alpha=alpha):.3f}')\nneed = tool.solve_power(d, power=0.80, alpha=alpha)\nprint(f'n per group for 80% power = {need:.1f}, so use {int(np.ceil(need))}')\nrng = np.random.default_rng(3)\na = rng.normal(0, sd, (5000, n)); b = rng.normal(diff, sd, (5000, n))\np = stats.ttest_ind(a, b, axis=1).pvalue\nprint(f'simulated power = {(p <= alpha).mean():.3f}')","answer":"d = 0.30. With 40 per group the exact power is 0.263, so the test would miss a real 3-minute gain about 74% of the time, and the simulation (0.264) agrees. For 80% power the team needs 175.4, so 176 visitors per group. A small effect needs a large sample, so the plan is too weak as it stands.","out":"effect size d = 0.30\npower with 40 per group = 0.263\nn per group for 80% power = 175.4, so use 176\nsimulated power = 0.264","figs":[]}}});
